5. what is aop
AOP stands for Aspect-Oriented Programming. Its primary purpose is to enhance the original business logic without introducing duplicate logic patterns
all over the codebase.
In practical development, we usually implement AOP in two different styles:
The first style is specific to the controller layer: An example of this is global exception handling, which we implement using the @RestControllerAdvice 
annotation.
The second style is a more generic aspect: We define a dedicated class decorated with the @Aspect annotation. When writing this type of aspect, the 
implementation always revolves around two critical dimensions: 'Where' and 'When'.
Where is defined by the @Pointcut, which explicitly specifies the target methods or annotations we want to intercept.
When is defined by the Advice, which dictates the exact timing of the execution. This includes five standard annotations: @Before, @After, @AfterReturning,
@AfterThrowing, and the most powerful one, @Around.
By linking 'When' and 'Where' together, AOP allows us to seamlessly separate cross-cutting concerns—like logging or security checks—from our core business 
logic.


