7. Describe Spring MVC
When describing Spring MVC, it's critical to clarify that it doesn't just refer to the standard 3-tier internal separation of Controller, Service, and DAO.
Instead, its core architectural purpose is to handle the precise routing and dispatching of HTTP requests at the web layer.
We can break down its workflow from the network level down to its core component pipeline:
First, how the request hits the server: When a client browser fires an HTTP request, it utilizes the IP address to locate the remote machine, and the 
unique Port number to bind to the specific application process. That is why port numbers must be unique within a single OS.
Second, the entrance through DispatcherServlet: Once the request hits the application, it immediately encounters the brain of Spring MVC—the 
DispatcherServlet, which acts as the Front Controller. In legacy systems, developers had to manually wire this up inside a painful web.xml configuration 
file. Modern Spring Boot has refactored all of this out into pre-configured boilerplate logic under the hood.
Finally, the routing mechanism via the Handler Chain: The DispatcherServlet acts as a coordinator. It hands the request over to the HandlerMapping, which 
maintains a Handler Chain containing all registered RESTful endpoints. The HandlerMapping looks at the combination of the URL and the HTTP Method (for 
example, GET /employee/1) to find a match. Once matched, it delegates the execution to the corresponding method in our Controller layer.
Once processed, the data is returned under @ResponseBody as a JSON payload, which is piped back to the client browser for rendering. That is the true 
essence and underlying flow of the Spring MVC framework.


8. how do you validate input data in spring boot
