10. how does spring mvc work
To explain exactly how Spring MVC works, the entire framework revolves around a highly coordinated data flow based on the Front Controller pattern.
Even though modern Spring Boot wraps this into auto-configuration so we don't have to wire it manually, understanding this underlying execution pipeline 
from scratch is crucial. It breaks down into four sequential steps:
Step 1: Request Interception. Every incoming HTTP request from the client first hits the central coordinator of the web layer, which is the 
DispatcherServlet.
Step 2: Routing Lookup. The DispatcherServlet does not execute business logic itself. Instead, it immediately consults the HandlerMapping. The 
HandlerMapping evaluates the combination of the incoming URL and HTTP method to scan the registered handler chains and pinpoint the exact controller 
method assigned to that route.
Step 3: Controller Execution. Once the correct endpoint is resolved, the DispatcherServlet utilizes an adapter to actually invoke that specific method 
within our Controller layer.
Step 4: Response Handling. In modern decoupled web services where we use @RestController, the controller returns a raw data object. Spring MVC 
automatically steps in with an HttpMessageConverter to serialize that object into a clean JSON response body, which the DispatcherServlet pipes directly 
back to the client browser.
Articulating this pipeline smoothly with the correct components demonstrates a rock-solid grasp of the Spring ecosystem that holds up under intense 
technical interview scrutiny.


