12. what is webflux? Have you used it in your 
When discussing WebFlux, I prefer to frame its role across three dimensions: its technical architecture, its reactive core components, and its industry 
evolution alongside Java 21.
First, The Technical Essence (Non-blocking Alternative):
WebFlux is an asynchronous, non-blocking reactive programming framework introduced in Spring 5 as an alternative to Spring MVC. While traditional Spring 
MVC relies on Tomcat's classic 'thread-per-request' synchronous model—where one thread handles one request from start to finish—WebFlux leverages the 
Reactor library. It treats incoming traffic as data streams flowing through channels managed by minimal worker thread groups, executing operations in 
highly parallel, non-blocking event loops.
Second, Core Components & Code Implementation (Mono & Flux):
In terms of code implementation, WebFlux completely shifts the return types. Instead of returning raw entities, everything is wrapped into reactive 
streams: a single object is wrapped as a Mono, whereas a group or list of objects is returned as a Flux. Working with it is highly similar to Java 8 
Stream APIs, where data must be processed through functional pipelines and actively subscribed to. I have hands-on experience exploring and utilizing 
it to handle data streams in high-concurrency, I/O-bound edge components.
Third, Production Pain Points & The Java 21 Evolution (Crucial Interview Insight):
However, in real-world enterprise development, a major operational drawback of WebFlux is that it is notoriously difficult to debug. Because the execution 
is asynchronous and stream-based, setting up standard breakpoints to track internal processing states becomes an absolute nightmare.
Furthermore, with the rollout of Java 21 introducing Virtual Threads, the performance barrier of the traditional thread model has been completely shattered.
Modern Tomcat setups paired with Virtual Threads allow us to achieve massive concurrent throughput using straightforward, readable Spring MVC code. Unless a
company has already heavily invested resources into a reactive infrastructure, the industry is shifting away from WebFlux toward the 'Java 21 Virtual 
Threads + Spring MVC' combo due to its vastly superior maintainability.


