16. how Spring IOC work , all annotations and injection and bean types
To thoroughly explain how Spring IOC works, it’s best to view it not as a static set of terms, but as a highly coordinated, automated lifecycle pipeline 
managed entirely by the application context:
Step 1: Metadata Bootstrapping & Scanning: Upon microservice startup, the @SpringBootApplication annotation triggers the component scanning mechanism. 
The IOC container actively reads metadata within the specified package paths, searching for classes decorated with bean stereotypes. This includes the 
generic @Component, or layer-specific annotations like @RestController, @Service, and @Repository. Alternatively, custom configurations can be declared 
inside a @Configuration class using method-level @Bean annotations. If certain components are explicitly blacklisted under the exclude attribute of the 
startup class, they are completely bypassed.
Step 2: Instantiation & Scope Resolution: Once these blueprints are gathered, the container processes their Bean Definitions and kicks off bean 
instantiation. By default, Spring beans adopt a Singleton scope, meaning the container creates and maintains only one shared instance globally for each 
bean definition. During this exact creation phase, if the system encounters an implicit circular dependency, we strategically apply the @Lazy annotation 
to defer initialization, gracefully unlinking the deadlock at runtime.
Step 3: Dependency Injection (DI): After objects are physically instantiated, the container executes dependency injection to stitch them together. While 
field and setter injections exist, our production standard strictly enforces Constructor Injection. In modern Spring versions, if a component exposes only 
a single constructor, the @Autowired annotation becomes entirely optional—the container automatically resolves and autowires the dependent beans, 
which significantly boosts code readability.
Framing Spring IOC around this end-to-end lifecycle demonstrates a mature, production-level engineering mindset that goes far beyond generic textboo 
definitions.


17. how many ways to inject bean in spring and which one we use most
In the Spring framework, there are three standard ways to execute dependency injection: Constructor Injection, Field Injection, and Setter Injection.
In modern production-level development, Constructor Injection has become the absolute industry standard and our team's go-to implementation. It’s important
to note that all three approaches are valid within the ecosystem, but constructor injection is heavily prioritized due to three massive architectural 
advantages:
First, it honors the 'Fail-Fast' engineering philosophy: This is the most crucial reason. If we rely on field injection, the Spring Boot application might
boot up successfully without any hitches, only to crash unexpectedly with a fatal NullPointerException (NPE) during runtime when that specific code path is
hit. Constructor injection shifts that risk. It enforces that all necessary dependencies must be available at the exact moment of instantiation; if a 
dependency is missing, the container will crash immediately during startup. This 'Fail-Fast' behavior allows us to capture integration gaps before the code 
ever leaves the local environment.
Second, it ensures NullPointerException (NPE) prevention: Because dependencies are tightly bound through the object's constructor, it guarantees that any 
instantiated Spring Bean is a fully formed, robust object that is permanently safe from un-injected runtime references.
Third, it is highly Unit-Test friendly: When writing standard unit tests using Mockito, we often want to isolate our logic without booting up a heavy, 
time-consuming Spring application context. With constructor injection, we can treat the component as a pure Java class, directly utilizing the new keyword 
to instantiate the object and passing our mocked dependencies straight into the constructor parameters.
Structuring the workflow around constructor injection creates a clean, predictable, and highly secure dependency pipeline across all our microservices


18. by name vs by type
When explaining how Spring resolves dependency injection, the relationship between byType and byName is best described as a structured, two-stage resolution
process:
Stage 1: byType is Always the First Priority:
When a dependency is declared in our components, the Spring IOC container always begins the lookup process by evaluating the class type (byType). It 
searches the context for a bean that matches the exact data type or interface requestedd. If exactly one matching bean is found, Spring injects it 
immediately, and the lookup succeeds.
Stage 2: Automatic Fallback to byName under Conflict:
If Stage 1 encounters a scenario where multiple candidate beans implement the exact same requested type or interface, a conflict occurs. To resolve this 
ambiguity, Spring automatically falls back on the byName mechanism. It attempts to match the variable field name in your code against the registered bean 
names in the IOC container.
Enterprise Best Practices for Overriding Ambiguity:
In real-world microservices—where we frequently have multiple implementations of a single interface (such as a VisaPaymentService and a 
StripePaymentService)—relying blindly on field names is risky. Therefore, we explicitly dictate control using two standard annotations:
@Primary: This is applied directly at the component class level, signaling the container to treat this specific implementation as the 
default choice if a type collision occurs.
@Qualifier: This is applied at the injection site (e.g., inside the constructor parameters), where we pass an explicit string name—like 
@Qualifier("stripePaymentService")—to bypass type matching entirely and force a surgical, name-based injection.
To summarize, Spring’s philosophy is 'byType first, then byName as a fallback', and as developers, we utilize @Primary and @Qualifier to maintain strict 
deterministic control over our bean wiring.


19. why constructor injection
In our production-grade enterprise development, we strictly enforce Constructor Injection because it perfectly embodies the principles of defensive 
programming and the 'Fail-Fast' engineering philosophy. I always summarize its benefits into three major architectural reasons:
1. It Guarantees Absolute Runtime NullPointerException (NPE) Prevention:
When developers rely on field injection, the Spring Boot application often boots up without any complaints, even if a dependency is completely missing 
or misconfigured. This creates a hidden time bomb that explodes as a fatal runtime NPE when that code path is hit by live traffic. Constructor injection 
prevents this at the JVM level. It dictates that a bean cannot physically be constructed without all its mandatory dependencies provided as arguments. This ensures that every bean managed in our container is always fully initialized, state-secure, and robust.
2. It Enforces the 'Fail-Fast' Principle at Startup:
From an operational safety standpoint, we always want bugs to surface at the earliest possible stage. With constructor injection, if there is a broken 
dependency chain or a missing bean mapping, Spring Boot will fail to bootstrap and crash immediately upon startup. This 'Fail-Fast' mechanism catches
architectural gaps directly on the developer's local machine or during the CI/CD build phase, completely preventing 'unstable' deployments from leaking into production.
3. It Minimizes Framework Coupling, Making Unit Testing Seamless:
When writing fast, isolated unit tests using Mockito, we want to avoid booting up a heavy, time-consuming Spring Application Context. Because constructor 
injection makes all dependencies highly explicit, the class behaves as a pure, loosely coupled Java object. We can seamlessly instantiate the target class 
using the plain new keyword in our test files and pass mocked dependencies directly into the constructor.
To put it simply, we prioritize constructor injection because it uses startup-stage failure (Fail-Fast) to guarantee runtime system stability


